#: ../../../env.rst:4
msgid "Environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:7
msgid "Basics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:9
msgid ""
"Environment variables are written as ``$`` followed by a name (e.g. "
"``$HOME``, ``$PWD``, ``$PATH``).  They can be set, deleted, and used just "
"like regular Python variables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:13
msgid ""
"@ $HOME\n"
"'/home/snail'\n"
"\n"
"@ $GOAL = 'Master the shell'\n"
"@ print($GOAL)\n"
"Master the shell\n"
"@ del $GOAL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:23
msgid "You can also build values from other variables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:25
msgid ""
"@ $NUM = \"123\"\n"
"@ $EXT = $NUM + \"456\"\n"
"@ $EXT\n"
"'123456'\n"
"@ $FNUM = \"{FILLME}456\".format(FILLME=$NUM)\n"
"@ $FNUM\n"
"'123456'\n"
"@ \"%s456\" % $NUM\n"
"'123456'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:39
msgid ""
"To update ``os.environ`` when the xonsh environment changes set :ref:"
"`$UPDATE_OS_ENVIRON <update_os_environ>` to ``True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:44
msgid "The Environment Itself ``@.env``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:46
msgid ""
"All environment variables live in the built-in ``@.env`` mapping. You can "
"access this mapping directly, but in most situations you don't need to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:50
msgid "To check whether a variable exists:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:52
msgid ""
"@ 'HOME' in @.env\n"
"True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:57
msgid "To get help on a specific variable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:59
msgid "@ @.env.help('XONSH_DEBUG')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:63
msgid "You can also set a variable on the command line before launching xonsh:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:65
msgid ""
"@ $HELLO='snail' xonsh -c 'echo Hello $HELLO'\n"
"Hello snail"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:72
msgid "Environment Lookup with ``${<expr>}``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:74
msgid ""
"``$NAME`` works when you know the variable name up front.  To construct the "
"name programmatically, use the ``${<expr>}`` operator -- any valid Python "
"expression can go inside the curly braces:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:78
msgid ""
"@ x = 'USER'\n"
"@ ${x}\n"
"'snail'\n"
"@ ${'HO' + 'ME'}\n"
"'/home/snail'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:88
msgid "Environment Types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:90
msgid ""
"Environment variables in xonsh are not limited to strings -- they can hold "
"any Python type: strings, numbers, lists, and arbitrary objects.  When a "
"variable is used as a subprocess argument, xonsh converts it to a string "
"automatically:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:95
msgid ""
"@ $MY_STR = 'hello'\n"
"@ $MY_NUM = 42\n"
"@ $MY_LIST = [1, 2, 3]\n"
"@ showcmd echo $MY_STR $MY_NUM $MY_LIST\n"
"['echo', 'hello', '42', '[1, 2, 3]']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:103
msgid ""
"``$PATH`` is an :class:`~xonsh.environ.EnvPath` object -- a special list "
"that makes it easy to add and remove directories:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:106
msgid ""
"@ $PATH\n"
"['/usr/local/bin', '/usr/bin', '/bin']\n"
"@ $PATH.append('/opt/mytools/bin')\n"
"@ $PATH.insert(0, '$HOME/.local/bin')\n"
"@ $PATH\n"
"['/home/snail/.local/bin', '/usr/local/bin', '/usr/bin', '/bin',\n"
"'/opt/mytools/bin']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:116
msgid ""
"Any variable whose name ends in ``PATH`` or ``DIRS`` is automatically "
"treated as an :class:`~xonsh.environ.EnvPath`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:119
msgid ""
"In subprocess mode, referencing an undefined environment variable will "
"produce an empty string.  In Python mode, however, a ``KeyError`` will be "
"raised if the variable does not exist in the environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:126
msgid "Xonsh Environment vs ``os.environ``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:128
msgid ""
"Xonsh maintains its own environment (``@.env``) that is separate from "
"Python's ``os.environ``.  The two differ in important ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:131
msgid ""
"**``os.environ``** is the standard OS process environment.  It only holds "
"**string** values and is inherited by child processes automatically.  "
"Libraries like ``subprocess``, ``os.system``, and any C code that calls "
"``getenv()`` all read from it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:136
msgid ""
"**``@.env``** is xonsh's rich environment.  It supports **typed values** -- "
"lists for ``PATH`` variables, ints, bools, and even arbitrary Python "
"objects.  It also provides defaults, validation, documentation, and the "
"``swap()`` context manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:141
msgid ""
"When you set ``$MY_VAR = [1, 2, 3]`` in xonsh, the value lives in ``@.env`` "
"as a Python list.  But ``os.environ`` knows nothing about it -- it still "
"holds whatever was there when xonsh started.  This means that child "
"processes launched from Python code (e.g. via ``subprocess.run()``) won't "
"see xonsh-side changes by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:147
msgid ""
"Subprocess commands launched through xonsh operators (``$()``, ``$[]``, "
"etc.) **do** see the xonsh environment because xonsh detypes and passes it "
"explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:152
msgid "``$UPDATE_OS_ENVIRON``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:154
msgid ""
"Set ``$UPDATE_OS_ENVIRON = True`` (default ``False``) to keep ``os.environ`` "
"in sync with ``@.env``.  When enabled, every change to the xonsh environment "
"is immediately written to ``os.environ`` (converted to a string).  This is "
"useful when you rely on third-party Python libraries that read ``os."
"environ`` directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:161
msgid ""
"$UPDATE_OS_ENVIRON = True\n"
"\n"
"$DATABASE_URL = 'postgres://localhost/mydb'\n"
"# Now os.environ['DATABASE_URL'] is also set,\n"
"# so libraries like sqlalchemy will pick it up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:169
msgid ""
"Note that setting ``$UPDATE_OS_ENVIRON = False`` only stops further "
"synchronization -- it does not revert ``os.environ`` to its original state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:175
msgid "Temporary Variables with ``swap``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:177
msgid ""
"Use ``@.env.swap()`` to set environment variables for the duration of a "
"``with`` block.  The original values are restored automatically when the "
"block exits, even if an exception is raised:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:181
msgid ""
"with @.env.swap(PGPASSWORD=@.imp.getpass.getpass('pgpass:')):\n"
"    for db in ['db1', 'db2']:\n"
"        psql postgresql://user@host:5439/@(db) -c 'select 1'\n"
"\n"
"# $PGPASSWORD is unset here"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:189
msgid "Multiple variables can be swapped at once:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:191
msgid ""
"with @.env.swap(LANG='C', LC_ALL='C'):\n"
"    sort data.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:197
msgid "Unsetting a Variable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:199
msgid ""
"Sometimes you need to remove an environment variable for a single command or "
"a single scope, not just override it. Assigning the sentinel ``@.env."
"DELETE_VAR`` does exactly that — the variable behaves as if it was never set "
"for the duration of the surrounding scope:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:204
msgid ""
"@ $HOSTNAME='myhost'\n"
"@ $HOSTNAME=@.env.DELETE_VAR env | grep -c '^HOSTNAME='\n"
"0\n"
"@ echo $HOSTNAME\n"
"myhost"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:212
msgid "The sentinel works on every path where a variable can be set:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:214
msgid ""
"# Inline prefix for a single subprocess\n"
"$HTTPS_PROXY=@.env.DELETE_VAR curl https://example.com\n"
"\n"
"# `swap` block — mask a variable for a multi-statement scope\n"
"with @.env.swap(LD_PRELOAD=@.env.DELETE_VAR):\n"
"    # Run something that misbehaves with LD_PRELOAD\n"
"\n"
"# Inside a callable alias — mask for any subprocess it spawns\n"
"@aliases.register\n"
"def _clean_env(env):\n"
"    env['HOSTNAME'] = @.env.DELETE_VAR\n"
"    # ...\n"
"\n"
"# In an `on_pre_spec_run` handler — mask per-spec at runtime\n"
"@events.on_pre_spec_run\n"
"def _strip_secrets(spec, **_):\n"
"    spec.env = (spec.env or {}) | {'SECRET_TOKEN': @.env.DELETE_VAR}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:234
msgid ""
"Reading a masked variable raises ``KeyError``, ``in`` returns ``False``, and "
"iteration / ``detype`` skip the key — so nothing leaks the sentinel into a "
"subprocess as a stringified value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:239
msgid "Callable Environment Variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:241
msgid ""
"In some cases you may want an environment variable with a dynamically "
"created value.  Define a class with a ``__repr__`` method and assign an "
"instance to the variable -- xonsh will call ``repr()`` every time the "
"variable is accessed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:246
msgid ""
"@ class Stamp:\n"
"     \"\"\"Return current date as string representation.\"\"\"\n"
"     def __repr__(self):\n"
"        return @.imp.datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@ $DT = Stamp()\n"
"@ $DT\n"
"2024-11-11T11:11:22\n"
"@ echo $DT\n"
"2024-11-11T11:11:33\n"
"@ env | grep DT\n"
"DT=2024-11-11T11:11:44"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:262
msgid ""
"Each access produces a fresh value, so the variable always reflects the "
"current state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:266
msgid "Registering Environment Variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:268
msgid ""
"You can manually register environment variables to define their type and "
"documentation. This is particularly useful for extensions or complex "
"configurations. The documentation provided will be shown during tab-"
"completion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:272
msgid ""
"@.env.register('MY_VAR1', type='int', default=1, doc='Demo variable 1.')\n"
"@.env.register('MY_VAR2', type='int', default=2, doc='Demo variable 2.')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:277
msgid "Now, when you type ``$MY_<Tab>``, you will see the description."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:279
msgid ""
"Available types: ``\"bool\"``, ``\"str\"``, ``\"int\"``, ``\"float\"``, "
"``\"path\"``, ``\"env_path\"``, ``\"abs_path\"``.  To get the current list:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:282
msgid ""
"@ list(@.imp.xonsh.environ.ENSURERS.keys())\n"
"['bool', 'str', 'path', 'env_path', 'abs_path', 'float', 'int', "
"'var_pattern']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:288
msgid "Getting Help on a Variable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:290
msgid ""
"Use the ``$VAR?`` syntax to see the description, default value, and other "
"metadata of any environment variable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:293
msgid ""
"@ $XONSH_COMMANDS_CACHE_READ_DIR_ONCE?\n"
"Name: $XONSH_COMMANDS_CACHE_READ_DIR_ONCE\n"
"Description: List of directory prefixes whose contents are cached on first\n"
"access and never re-read within the session.\n"
"Default: []\n"
"Configurable: True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:302
msgid "This works for both built-in and registered variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:305
msgid "Variable Patterns (``VarPattern``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:307
msgid ""
"Xonsh allows defining **pattern rules** that automatically apply type "
"handling to environment variables whose names match a regex pattern.  This "
"is powered by the ``VarPattern`` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:312
msgid "Built-in patterns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:314
msgid "Xonsh ships with two default patterns:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:316
msgid ""
"``$XONSH_ENV_PATTERN_PATH`` -- variables ending with ``PATH`` are treated as "
"``env_path`` (e.g. ``$MYPATH``, ``$LD_LIBRARY_PATH``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:318
msgid ""
"``$XONSH_ENV_PATTERN_DIRS`` -- variables ending with ``DIRS`` are treated as "
"``env_path`` (e.g. ``$XDG_DATA_DIRS``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:322
msgid "Creating a pattern"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:324
msgid "Set a ``VarPattern`` value directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:326
msgid ""
"$XONSH_ENV_PATTERN_NUM = @.imp.xonsh.environ.VarPattern(r\"\\w*_NUM$\", "
"\"int\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:330
msgid "Or register with a default value and documentation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:332
msgid ""
"@.env.register(\n"
"    \"XONSH_ENV_PATTERN_NUM\",\n"
"    type=\"var_pattern\",\n"
"    default=@.imp.xonsh.environ.VarPattern(r\"\\w*_NUM$\", \"int\"),\n"
"    doc=\"Pattern rule: env vars matching *_NUM are treated as int.\",\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:342
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:344
msgid ""
"Once a pattern is active, matching variables are automatically converted:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:346
msgid ""
"@ $QWE_NUM = '42'\n"
"@ type($QWE_NUM)\n"
"int"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:353
msgid "Excluding variables from a pattern"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:355
msgid ""
"Some variables may match a pattern but have different meaning.  Add them to "
"the ``exclude`` list:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:358
msgid ""
"@ $XONSH_ENV_PATTERN_DIRS.exclude.append('JUPYTER_PLATFORM_DIRS')\n"
"@ $JUPYTER_PLATFORM_DIRS = '1'\n"
"@ $JUPYTER_PLATFORM_DIRS\n"
"'1'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:366
msgid "Disabling patterns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:368
msgid "Set a pattern variable to ``None`` to disable it entirely:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../env.rst:370
msgid ""
"$XONSH_ENV_PATTERN_DIRS = None\n"
"$XONSH_ENV_PATTERN_PATH = None"
msgstr ""

